# Chapter 7 — Symmetry Breaking and Disequilibrium

*A balanced arrangement can be a starting point rather than a destination.*

Place equal numbers of grains on either side of a divider and shake the container. The symmetry is easy to see. Whether it survives depends on motion, collisions, and the barrier between the compartments. This chapter asks how material processes develop differences and what those differences enable.

Ontological uncertainty concerns the proposed foundation of reality. Here the questions are more specific: which states are stable, which perturbations grow, which quantities are conserved, and which gradients sustain change? These mechanisms let us investigate a natural history of differentiation without treating every asymmetry as the same physical phenomenon.

Consider an idealized model of a magnet. At each site, a small magnetic variable can point in one of two opposite directions. Neighboring variables favor alignment. The rules are unchanged if every direction is reversed. At sufficiently high temperature the system lacks a net preferred direction; below a critical temperature, a large system can develop collective magnetization. The resulting state can have less symmetry than the rule describing its possible behavior.[1]

The example reinforces a distinction used throughout the book. A symmetric rule need not produce a state that displays the entire symmetry of that rule. Particular outcomes require particular states. If two directions are equally allowed, the existence of a magnetized state does not require a law that declares one of those directions universally superior.

This is spontaneous symmetry breaking in an idealized setting. Real samples add boundaries, imperfections, magnetic fields, domains, and finite sizes. Those details matter to which state appears and how long it remains. Their presence does not erase the distinction between the symmetry of the description and the organization of the material.

What has the magnet acquired? It has acquired a collective condition under which subsequent interactions occur. A nearby probe can respond to an organization that cannot be assigned to one constituent considered alone. What became possible depended on the constituents, their interactions, and the conditions under which their collective state formed.

There is no need for an organizing mind outside the material. But there is also no creation from a complete absence of rules. The model already contains the possible directions, interactions, and thermal conditions. It explains how a particular organization arises from those conditions. It does not explain why those are the conditions of existence itself.

That limitation is also a source of strength. The explanation has a boundary. Within it, we can ask what changes when the temperature changes, when an external field is applied, or when the interactions are altered. The account earns its authority through those distinctions rather than through the size of the word emergence.

A symmetric arrangement is not necessarily a stable arrangement. This distinction can be tested with actual populations of particles. Prepare comparable numbers on two sides of a container and ask whether small departures from equality shrink or grow. Occasional differences occur in many fluctuating systems; the more consequential event is a persistent separation into a crowded side and a sparse side.

In experiments reported in 2001, Ko van der Weele and colleagues used 600 glass beads in a vertically shaken container divided into two equal compartments. Beads could cross the dividing wall. Strong shaking supported an approximately even distribution. Reducing the driving could destabilize that distribution and leave most beads on one side.[2] The apparatus did not need a built-in choice of which side should win.

The mechanism depends on the material. These are macroscopic grains whose collisions dissipate kinetic energy, rather than the ideal elastic particles of an equilibrium gas. A denser compartment has more collisions, slowing its grains and reducing their ability to cross the barrier. A small excess can consequently reduce outward escape enough to draw in still more particles. Jens Eggers developed a quantitative account of this instability and compared it with numerical simulations.[3]

The decisive relation concerns escape rates. If adding particles increases the rate at which they leave, the resulting outflow can restore balance. If adding particles reduces the escape rate in the relevant regime, the same initial excess can grow. Equality remains a possible mathematical solution, but the response to a disturbance has changed. Stability belongs to the dynamics around a state, not merely to the appearance of the state itself.

An exactly balanced deterministic equation does not have to leave its balanced solution without a perturbation. Real equal-count preparations still contain moving grains, differing velocities, and fluctuations in crossings. They do not specify a perfectly symmetric microscopic history. Once the balanced state is unstable, those small differences can decide which asymmetric outcome develops. In an ideally unbiased set of repetitions, either side can be favored equally often even though individual runs become strongly uneven.

The final populations need not continue diverging without limit. A stationary asymmetric state can balance the number crossing in each direction while retaining unequal numbers on the two sides. Equal flows are a different condition from equal populations. The history has produced an organization in which the same exchange rules sustain a different distribution.

The supply of energy belongs inside this explanation. Shaking sustains motion while collisions transfer energy into other degrees of freedom. Clustering is compatible with dissipation in the complete apparatus; spatial order among the grains is not a demonstration that total thermodynamic entropy has decreased. The experiment also gives a concrete counterexample to the idea that simply increasing the drive must always break symmetry: sufficiently vigorous shaking sustains the balanced regime.

For the book, the important possibility is that differentiation need not be specified as an initial inventory of differences. A material process can amplify a fluctuation, and the resulting distribution changes the conditions of later exchange. The formal symmetry of the arrangement permits equivalent alternatives; the physical dynamics determines whether the balanced state persists. The system is jointly material and Platonic in the sense developed throughout this argument.

This also separates the predictability of a kind of outcome from the predictability of its particular direction. Persistent imbalance can be expected in an unstable regime without a universal preference for left over right. The distinction offers a useful question for our comparison with discovery: could a class of transition become robust while its particular realization remains contingent?

The proposed common driver remains disequilibrium acting through accessible constraints. Here dissipation is part of a demonstrated mechanism of instability. Establishing that the resulting state maximizes an appropriately defined entropy-production rate would require an additional comparison. The experiment gives us a physical route from balance to differentiation, rather than a universal rule that every symmetry must disappear.

The Higgs mechanism brings a related distinction into particle physics, although the magnetic analogy must be handled carefully. The electroweak theory describes a Higgs field whose nonzero vacuum value is associated with the masses of the W and Z bosons; interactions with the field also enter the account of elementary fermion masses. The field's state affects how other fields behave. The 2012 ATLAS and CMS observations of a new boson supplied crucial experimental support for the Higgs picture.[4]

This is not a claim that every particle became massive through the same process, nor that all mass is a gift from a single particle. The underlying theory is also subtler than arrows in a magnet: gauge symmetries include redundancies in description, so different mathematical orientations need not represent physically different worlds. What experiments test are observable consequences, including particles, interactions, and relations among measured quantities.

The relevant lesson is that properties can depend on the state of other physical structure. The account does not begin with a list of independent objects, each carrying all its attributes in isolation. Some attributes are understood through interactions and a background state shared across the theory.

A background is still something whose role can be investigated.

The granular and magnetic examples explain how a state can cease to display a symmetry of its governing description. The universe also contains asymmetries involving matter and antimatter, left and right, and past and future. To investigate their common place in a material history, we must identify what changes in each case: a state, an interaction, an abundance, or the conditions under which a process unfolds.

The historical program brings five questions together. Why does the observable universe contain predominantly matter rather than antimatter? Why do weak interactions distinguish left from right? Why does space have its observed three large dimensions? Why do macroscopic processes possess a direction in time? And were physical laws and universal constants themselves assembled?

The book proposes that these asymmetries and structural features reflect historical processes. An apparent initial condition would become an outcome requiring its own material explanation. Symmetry breaking supplies one concrete mechanism by which a physical state acquires distinctions, while the larger program asks whether the interactions, dimensional organization, temporal conditions, and parameter relations also have developmental origins. Each answer must identify what actually changes. A history of abundance, a history of an interaction, and a history of spacetime impose different demands on the account.[5]

The proposed connection is cumulative. A physical differentiation can alter subsequent interactions; the resulting organization can stabilize some configurations and change the pathways available to others. Some developments can also create or maintain gradients through which further transformations occur. The theory therefore seeks a history in which asymmetries, accessible pathways, and the conditions driving assembly develop together. It must determine which asymmetries perform this energetic role: an unequal population or a preferred handedness is not by itself a quantified thermodynamic gradient.

Consider left and right. Parity concerns whether the laws remain unchanged under a spatial reflection. A particular asymmetric object does not establish parity violation: a right-handed screw can exist in a world whose laws also permit its mirror image. The issue is whether the physical process itself respects the reflected description. In 1957, Chien-Shiung Wu and her collaborators reported an experimental violation of parity in cobalt-60 beta decay.[6]

That result is different from a symmetric magnetic model acquiring a particular orientation. In one case, the question concerns the symmetry of the interactions; in the other, a state fails to display a symmetry allowed by the model. Calling both a preference of nature would conceal the difference we need to understand.

The matter–antimatter problem adds another distinction. Charge conjugation exchanges particles with their antiparticles; combining that operation with spatial reflection gives CP. Experiments have shown that CP is not an exact symmetry of all particle processes. The 1964 neutral-kaon work of Christenson, Cronin, Fitch, and Turlay was a landmark in establishing this. Yet the existence of CP violation is not, on its own, a complete account of the cosmic excess of matter.[7]

The research has continued into particles of the class that includes protons and neutrons. In 2025, the LHCb collaboration reported CP violation in a particular decay of the beauty baryon Λ_b⁰ and its antimatter counterpart. The comparison accounted for differences in production and detection before identifying an asymmetry in the decay probabilities.[8] It established unequal behavior in specified decay routes, rather than a difference in total lifetimes or the production of the cosmic matter surplus from equal initial populations.

To see the additional problem, consider the bookkeeping of pair creation and annihilation. Creating one baryon and one antibaryon increases both counts equally. Annihilating a pair reduces both equally. Those operations alone preserve their difference. If the initial net number is zero, simply repeating them cannot turn it into a net excess of baryons. A local fluctuation or a separation into different regions also leaves a global conserved difference unchanged.

Baryogenesis asks how a net excess could be generated dynamically. Under the standard assumptions of the Sakharov account, this requires processes that violate baryon-number conservation, violations of C and CP symmetry, and departure from thermal equilibrium.[7] The first permits the net quantity to change; the second permits a preference between corresponding processes; the third allows an asymmetry to develop outside the equilibrium cancellation. Their presence is a set of requirements, not a calculation of the observed surplus.

The role of disequilibrium makes this research especially relevant to the book. The early universe supplies a changing physical setting in which rates of expansion and interaction matter. An initially balanced abundance need not be the final one if the necessary processes operate. But departure from equilibrium and maximization of entropy production are different claims. The latter must still identify a measurable tendency and show how it generates the required abundance through specified interactions.

There is also a question about spatial distribution. Perhaps the observable excess is local and distant regions contain compensating antimatter. That possibility resembles the two-compartment illustration only superficially: matter and antimatter can annihilate where they meet. In their study of a cosmological patchwork, Andrew Cohen, Álvaro De Rújula, and Sheldon Glashow calculated the consequences of annihilation near domain boundaries. Their predicted gamma-ray signal constrained such a patchwork under the cosmological assumptions they investigated.[9]

This turns distribution into an observable question. A proposed separation has to explain the radiation its boundaries would produce, as well as the matter found in the region we inhabit. The calculation is not a census of everything beyond the observable universe. It shows why moving the missing antimatter elsewhere cannot, by itself, complete the explanation.

These cases have distinct explanatory tasks. The grains redistribute a conserved particle total. CP measurements establish unequal probabilities for corresponding particle processes. Baryogenesis models seek a net baryon excess, while domain models must confront the consequences of spatial separation. Together they make the development of asymmetry a substantive physical research program. They do not turn all asymmetries into one already-solved problem.

The claim worth carrying forward is that a balanced description need not specify the state that history will preserve. We should investigate which symmetric states are stable, which disturbances grow, which quantities can actually change, and which resulting structures become conditions for subsequent assembly. This is how a proposed history of physical differentiation can become more than an analogy.

For our argument, the distinction is productive. Some differences may arise through the development of a state. Others reveal properties of interactions. Some may reflect boundary conditions whose deeper origin remains unknown. A material history must discover which kind of explanation fits each case.

The observed number of large spatial dimensions poses a further question. We navigate three independent spatial directions, while physical time plays a different role in our descriptions. It is possible to ask whether this organization is fundamental, whether additional dimensions might be inaccessible at ordinary scales, or whether dimensionality could arise in a deeper theory. Asking those questions does not show that a process of assembly has selected three dimensions.

Even a proposed argument about the stability of structures in other dimensions has assumptions. Which laws are carried into the alternative world? How do interactions change with geometry? What counts as a stable structure? Changing one feature while freezing every other feature can be a useful investigation, but the resulting counterfactual belongs to a model. It is not a survey of all possible universes.

We should resist turning our familiarity with this world into knowledge of the frequency with which other worlds could occur.

Time presents an even more intimate asymmetry. We remember an opened door, not the opening that has yet to happen. Heat spreads from a hot object into cooler surroundings; we do not ordinarily see the dispersed energy spontaneously reassemble the original temperature difference. Many everyday processes have a direction even when the microscopic equations used to model them permit reversed motions.

A simple gas illustrates the statistical idea. Confine it to one side of a container and remove the partition. For ordinary macroscopic conditions, it spreads. There are vastly more microscopic arrangements compatible with a roughly even distribution than with every molecule crowded back into the original side. Boltzmann's account relates entropy to the multiplicity of microscopic states compatible with a macroscopic description. The initial condition is essential: we began with the gas confined.[10]

This is not a failure of the molecules to retrace their steps because they have forgotten the past. It is a statement about microscopic possibilities, typical behavior, and the conditions under which we began the comparison. Nor does microscopic chaos alone explain the direction. A model can be highly sensitive to initial conditions without that sensitivity selecting which temporal direction carries the low-entropy boundary.

The explanation therefore returns us to an inheritance. Why was there a partition? Who or what prepared the confined gas? In the laboratory we can follow the preparation into the work performed by an apparatus and into its environment. In cosmology the corresponding question reaches toward the universe's low-entropy past. A statistical account of relaxation does not, by itself, explain the origin of that past.

It is also not enough to say that the early universe was hot. Temperature is not entropy, and gravity complicates the inference from visual uniformity to maximal disorder. We cannot identify the entropy of the whole gravitational world merely by looking at the thermal character of one component. The problem of the initial condition remains a physical problem, not a defect to be removed by an evocative word.

We have reached a recurring pattern: a mechanism explains what happens given certain conditions, then leaves us asking how those conditions arose. That is not an infinite sequence of failures. It is often the shape of successful inquiry. The work is to make each dependence explicit and see whether a further mechanism can account for it.

For this book, thermodynamics supplies both possibility and restraint. Local organization can be maintained in an open system exchanging energy and matter with its surroundings. But entropy, energy, and power are different quantities. Energy has units of joules; power has units of joules per second; thermodynamic entropy has units of joules per kelvin. A claim about one cannot be transferred to another merely because each appears in a discussion of heat.[11]

A reservoir can hold a great deal of usable energy while the rate at which a particular system accesses it remains small. A barrier can prevent a transformation, even when the final state would be energetically favorable. Conversely, an increased rate of energy use can be spent wastefully. None of these possibilities contradicts conservation of energy or supplies a universal scale of progress.

Suppose a device depends on a temperature difference. Removing that difference can destroy its ability to do useful work even though the energy has not vanished. The capacity belonged to a relation between parts of the system. The history of establishing, exploiting, and dissipating that relation matters.

The cosmological hypothesis extends the proposed role of disequilibrium to the formation of its own conditions. Physical differences permit transformations; the proposed tendency toward maximum entropy-production rates concerns how activity develops through accessible pathways. A natural history of assembly must explain how those differences arise or persist, as well as how pathways form. It cannot assume that every asymmetric arrangement supplies a usable gradient.

That extends the theory beyond explaining what an engine does after someone supplies its reservoirs. The origins of the reservoirs, gradients, and constraints become part of the same inquiry. At cosmic scales, the hypothesis must specify which physical difference drives which transformation, which alternatives are accessible, and what entropy-production rate is being compared. The restricted principles discussed in chapter 2 do not already establish this universal extension.[11]

Symmetry breaking alone cannot establish disequilibrium. A system can have a state with less symmetry and still be in thermodynamic equilibrium.[1] Nor does positive entropy production establish that its rate is maximal. The proposal therefore requires two connected explanations: how the relevant development generates or preserves gradients, and whether the realized pathways follow the proposed rate principle. The low-entropy past remains among the conditions to explain. These requirements give the historical proposal a way to fail as well as a direction to pursue.

A newly formed bound structure need not continuously dissipate energy at the rate involved in its formation. Some products can remain after the process that made them has ended. The energetic history of assembly and the present activity of its product are different objects of explanation. A stable atom, a star exchanging energy, and an operating engine cannot simply be assigned the same kind of rate because all have histories.

This distinction prevents a mistaken version of the universal claim: that every object must visibly consume more at every moment. The stronger developmental question concerns the physical processes through which accessible structures arise, persist, and become conditions for later structures. If maximum entropy production governs some of those processes, its role must be derived at the appropriate level, including cases whose products are comparatively inactive.

The distinction matters to the account of life and to the human and technological histories developed later in the book. An organization may expand by opening a resource, persist by reducing its needs, or fail because its rate of exploitation destroys its support. Which happens is a question about its mechanism and environment. The arrow of time does not promise that every later arrangement will command more energy than every earlier one.

The granular result is a controlled laboratory experiment, not a natural experiment assigned by an external historical event. The published research supplies evidence for a particular instability; this manuscript has not reanalyzed its raw measurements. Its evidence record therefore separates the reported mechanism from a new estimate. A test of the proposed entropy-production maximum would additionally need measurements of the complete system and accessible alternatives.[12]

A differentiated state can become a condition of later events. The remaining question is whether some conditions we call laws or constants could themselves be outcomes of development. Chapter 8 takes up that conjecture, distinguishing the formation of organization within known laws from an explanation of those laws.

